{parent=i}

The first variation of the graph area functional is
$$
\frac d{dt}A(u+t\phi)\bigg|_{t=0}
=\int_{B_1}\frac{Du\cdot D\phi}{\sqrt{1+|Du|^2}}.
$$
Thus the <minimal surface equation for a graph> is
$$
\operatorname{div}\frac{Du}{\sqrt{1+|Du|^2}}=0,
$$
or, in nondivergence form,
$$
\boxed{\left(\delta_{ij}-\frac{D_iuD_ju}{1+|Du|^2}\right)D_{ij}u=0.}
$$
Differentiate the divergence equation with respect to $x_k$. Then $w=D_ku$ satisfies
$$
D_i(a^{ij}(Du)D_jw)=0,
$$
where
$$
\boxed{a^{ij}(p)=\frac{\delta_{ij}}{\sqrt{1+|p|^2}}
-\frac{p_ip_j}{{(1+|p|^2)}^{3/2}}.}
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

If $|Du|\leq L$, the eigenvalues of $a^{ij}(Du)$ lie between $(1+L^2)^{-3/2}$ and $1$, so the differentiated equations are uniformly elliptic with bounded measurable coefficients depending only on $L$. Apply the <De Giorgi-Nash-Moser theorem> to each $D_ku$. Since $|D_ku|\leq L$, for every $\theta<1$,
$$
[Du]_{C^{0,\alpha}(B_\theta)}\leq C(n,L,\theta)
$$
for some $\alpha=\alpha(n,L)\in(0,1)$. The map
$$
p\longmapsto\delta_{ij}-\frac{p_ip_j}{1+|p|^2}
$$
is smooth with bounded derivative on $|p|\leq L$. Composition therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\|b^{ij}\|_{C^{0,\alpha}(B_\theta)}\leq\beta(n,L,\theta).}
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The function $u$ solves the linear nondivergence equation
$$
b^{ij}(x)D_{ij}u=0.
$$
The coefficient matrix is uniformly elliptic, and part (ii) makes it $C^{0,\alpha}$ on every smaller ball. Solve the corresponding linear Dirichlet problem on $B_r\Subset B_1$ with boundary value $u$. Existence gives a $C^{2,\alpha}$ solution, and the <weak maximum principle for elliptic operators> gives uniqueness, so that solution is $u$. Hence $u\in C^{2,\alpha}_{\mathrm{loc}}(B_1)$.

The local boundedness estimate for uniformly elliptic equations first controls $\|u\|_{C^0(B_r)}$ by $\|u\|_{L^2(B_1)}$. Applying the <interior Schauder estimate> and covering $B_\theta$ by finitely many balls gives
$$
\boxed{\|u\|_{C^{2,\alpha}(B_\theta)}
\leq C(n,L,\theta)\|u\|_{L^2(B_1)}.}
$$

